# External fetch budget

Applies to budget-bearing PaperLab subagents when fetching external
content during a chat. Goal: keep tutor / explainer / comparator /
critic turns from spiraling into open-ended literature crawls
without proportional value.

## Who carries the budget

- **Budget-bearers:** `tutor`, `explainer`, `comparator`, `critic`.
- **Not budget-bearers:** `acquirer`, `dissector`, `experimenter`,
  `coder`, `evaluator`. These work from already-gated upstream
  artifacts; their external fetches (if any) are deterministic, not
  exploratory.

## What counts as a fetch

- ✅ **Counts:** `firecrawl` MCP calls, `WebSearch`, `WebFetch`.
- ❌ **Does not count:** cache hits (e.g.
  `papers/<slug>/.cache/citations/`), local file reads,
  `tools/pdf.extract_pdf_text` (extracts a local PDF).

## Budget

- **Per session:** ≤ 20 external fetches across all budget-bearers.
- **Per paper-bound task:** ≤ 7. A task is one durable artifact —
  one `<concept>-<slug>.md` write, one `<concept>.md`, one
  `comparison.md`, one `critic_reviews.md` / `code_review.md`, one
  tutor concept turn, one synthesis.

The agent **self-tracks** both counters. There is no hook
enforcement (rules are advisory in Cursor); this is honest
self-discipline.

## What to do at a threshold

When either counter reaches its cap, **stop before the next fetch**
and ask the user:

> "I've used `<N>` external fetches on this task / `<M>` this
> session, hitting the `<task|session>` budget. Continue with more,
> or stop here and answer from what we have?"

Wait for explicit confirmation. On confirmation, **both counters
reset** to zero — this is a checkpoint, not a hard cap. Without
confirmation, the agent stops fetching and answers from what it
already has.

The checkpoint exists to surface the cost to the user, not to
forbid further work.

## Preferred fetch order

In priority order, prefer the cheapest sufficient source. Walk the
list top-to-bottom; only escalate when the previous tier is
insufficient for the current claim.

1. **For paper claims:** the paper's text (`tools/pdf.extract_pdf_text`)
   and `spec.md` first. The dissector's extraction is the canonical
   source for *that paper*; do not fetch external blogs to re-explain
   content `spec.md` already covers. (These reads do **not** count
   against the budget — see "What counts as a fetch.")
2. **arXiv abstract** (cheap, structured) — for background or
   general field framing.
3. **One blog or tutorial** — accessible general framing, only when
   the paper / spec is insufficient.
4. **Author or lab page** — for canonical metadata or links.

**Never crawl whole sites.** No "fetch all pages on the lab's docs
subdomain" sprees, no recursive link-following.

## Reporting

When a budget-bearer reports back to the user (or to the calling
agent for backend invocations), include the fetch counts in the
report when any external fetch happened — e.g. *"Fetches: 3 task / 8
session."* When zero external fetches were made, omit the line.

## Rationale

Tutor and explainer turns can spiral into fetch-heavy explorations
that cost latency without proportional value. The per-task cap
protects individual turns; the per-session cap protects the chat as
a whole. A soft, reset-on-confirm checkpoint lets the user steer
without imposing a hard ceiling.
